1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,400
Block two.

2
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:04,375
How do we know a model learned anything?

3
00:00:04,375 --> 00:00:09,275
On the page, this block is the section "How do we know a model learned anything?

4
00:00:09,275 --> 00:00:12,150
Evaluation and generalisation".

5
00:00:12,150 --> 00:00:18,275
This is the centre of the session, and it is the part I would most like you to remember in five years.

6
00:00:18,275 --> 00:00:20,750
Three data sets and why.

7
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:23,625
The metrics, and what each one hides.

8
00:00:23,625 --> 00:00:33,650
And then leakage: the ways that information about your test set gets into your model without anybody intending it, which in our field happens mostly through homology.

9
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:38,950
A story first, because it is the clearest possible statement of the problem.

10
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:43,925
The story goes that a neural network was trained to spot tanks in photographs.

11
00:00:43,925 --> 00:00:47,475
On the training data it was essentially perfect.

12
00:00:47,475 --> 00:00:50,875
On new photographs it was no better than guessing.

13
00:00:50,875 --> 00:01:00,950
When people went back and looked at the data, they found that every photograph containing a tank had been taken on a sunny day, and every photograph without one on a cloudy day.

14
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:04,175
The network had learned to classify the weather.

15
00:01:04,175 --> 00:01:13,850
I should say that the story is probably apocryphal, or at least has never been pinned to a specific documented case, and you will find it told a dozen different ways.

16
00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:21,625
I tell it anyway, because the failure it describes is completely real and I have seen versions of it in biology more than once.

17
00:01:21,625 --> 00:01:32,625
The model finds the easiest thing in your data that predicts the label, and if that easiest thing is an artefact of how the data was collected, it will learn the artefact with great enthusiasm.

18
00:01:32,625 --> 00:01:41,825
Real versions in our field: all the tumour samples sequenced in one laboratory and all the controls in another, so the model learns the laboratory.

19
00:01:41,825 --> 00:01:47,450
All the positive examples taken from one database with a particular curation style.

20
00:01:47,450 --> 00:01:51,375
All the structures of one class solved by one technique.

21
00:01:51,375 --> 00:01:54,375
The lesson is not about networks at all.

22
00:01:54,375 --> 00:02:03,875
It is that your dataset has to be representative of the situation you intend to use the model in, and that nothing in the training procedure will tell you if it is not.

23
00:02:03,875 --> 00:02:06,350
Only looking at the data will.

24
00:02:06,950 --> 00:02:11,575
Now the standard machinery, and the reason there are three sets and not two.

25
00:02:11,575 --> 00:02:14,725
The training set is what you fit the weights on.

26
00:02:14,725 --> 00:02:16,675
That is obvious.

27
00:02:16,675 --> 00:02:20,025
The validation set is for making choices.

28
00:02:20,025 --> 00:02:22,700
How many epochs to train for.

29
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:24,900
How many hidden units.

30
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:27,350
Which features to include.

31
00:02:27,350 --> 00:02:30,000
Which of three models to prefer.

32
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,750
You try the options, you look at the validation score, and you take the best.

33
00:02:34,750 --> 00:02:39,575
That is entirely legitimate and it is what the validation set is for.

34
00:02:39,575 --> 00:02:45,825
The test set is looked at once, at the very end, to report what your chosen model actually achieves.

35
00:02:45,825 --> 00:02:51,975
And here is the reason for the separation, which is the bit that is easy to say and hard to feel.

36
00:02:51,975 --> 00:02:58,525
Every single choice you make by looking at the validation score makes that score a little bit optimistic.

37
00:02:58,525 --> 00:03:03,300
You are, in effect, fitting your choices to that particular set of examples.

38
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:14,150
If you try fifty configurations and keep the one that scored best on validation, part of what you selected for is real quality and part of it is luck with those examples.

39
00:03:14,150 --> 00:03:20,675
So the validation score of your chosen model is not an honest estimate of how it will do on new data.

40
00:03:20,675 --> 00:03:25,175
It is biased upward, and the harder you searched, the more biased it is.

41
00:03:25,175 --> 00:03:29,900
The test set is clean only because you did not use it to decide anything.

42
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:42,050
The moment you look at the test score, and then go back and change your model because you did not like it, your test set has quietly become a second validation set, and you no longer have an honest estimate at all.

43
00:03:42,050 --> 00:03:45,950
That is a rule about discipline, not about statistics.

44
00:03:45,950 --> 00:03:48,650
The arithmetic cannot save you.

45
00:03:49,250 --> 00:03:56,400
Here is that abstract point as a concrete decision, and I want you to make the decision yourself before I give the answer.

46
00:03:56,400 --> 00:04:01,175
The plot shows validation accuracy against the number of training epochs.

47
00:04:01,175 --> 00:04:06,450
It rises quickly, then wobbles along a plateau at about nought point eight nine.

48
00:04:06,450 --> 00:04:11,050
The single best epoch is epoch three, with nought point nine four.

49
00:04:11,050 --> 00:04:13,725
Which epoch would you choose, and why?

50
00:04:13,725 --> 00:04:15,825
Pause and decide.

51
00:04:15,825 --> 00:04:19,700
Most people say epoch three, because it is the highest.

52
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:22,725
Let me argue that it is the wrong answer.

53
00:04:22,725 --> 00:04:25,775
We have a hundred validation examples.

54
00:04:25,775 --> 00:04:36,675
With a hundred examples, a single accuracy figure is uncertain by roughly plus or minus nought point zero three, just from the luck of which hundred examples you happened to have.

55
00:04:36,675 --> 00:04:44,250
So the peak at nought point nine four and the plateau at nought point eight nine are about one and a half standard errors apart.

56
00:04:44,250 --> 00:04:48,375
That is not evidence that epoch three is genuinely better.

57
00:04:48,375 --> 00:04:53,525
Worse: we did not pick one number, we picked the maximum of fifty noisy numbers.

58
00:04:53,525 --> 00:04:58,225
The maximum of fifty noisy draws is systematically higher than the truth.

59
00:04:58,225 --> 00:05:07,575
That is selection bias, and it is the same phenomenon as the optimistic validation score on the previous slide, concentrated into one decision.

60
00:05:07,575 --> 00:05:14,325
So the rule I would give you is this: take the earliest epoch that is clearly on the plateau, not the single best one.

61
00:05:14,325 --> 00:05:16,350
Here, two epochs.

62
00:05:16,350 --> 00:05:22,350
You get essentially the same quality, you train faster, and you have not selected a lucky bump.

63
00:05:22,350 --> 00:05:24,550
And then the honest part.

64
00:05:24,550 --> 00:05:29,875
Having chosen two epochs on the validation set, we look at the test set, once.

65
00:05:29,875 --> 00:05:32,400
Nought point eight nine five.

66
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:39,400
Notice that is close to the plateau and well below the lucky peak, which is exactly what the argument predicted.

67
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:43,300
One last detail from this plot that is worth noticing.

68
00:05:43,300 --> 00:05:50,300
The training accuracy never settles down either; it bounces between nought point eight eight and nought point nine eight.

69
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:52,300
That is not a bug.

70
00:05:52,300 --> 00:06:00,800
The patterns have noise added, and five flat boundaries cannot separate noisy patterns perfectly, so the model keeps getting jostled.

71
00:06:01,400 --> 00:06:04,925
Accuracy tells you how often a classifier is wrong.

72
00:06:04,925 --> 00:06:11,275
The confusion matrix tells you what it is wrong about, which is almost always the more useful question.

73
00:06:11,275 --> 00:06:13,050
It is a grid.

74
00:06:13,050 --> 00:06:16,825
Rows are the truth, columns are what the model predicted.

75
00:06:16,825 --> 00:06:22,850
The cell in row i, column j counts how many examples of true class i were called class j.

76
00:06:22,850 --> 00:06:31,175
The diagonal is the correct answers; everything off the diagonal is a mistake, labelled with exactly what kind of mistake it was.

77
00:06:31,175 --> 00:06:41,725
Now look at ours, and remember what we predicted when we set up the problem: the cross shares pixels with the horizontal and the vertical bar, so that is where the confusion should be.

78
00:06:41,725 --> 00:06:43,625
And there it is.

79
00:06:43,625 --> 00:06:46,250
Nine crosses called vertical.

80
00:06:46,250 --> 00:06:49,875
Four horizontals and two verticals called cross.

81
00:06:49,875 --> 00:06:53,875
Almost every mistake in the whole matrix involves the cross.

82
00:06:53,875 --> 00:06:56,650
The model is not randomly bad.

83
00:06:56,650 --> 00:07:01,900
It is specifically confused in the one place where the classes genuinely overlap.

84
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:08,000
That is a model behaving sensibly on a hard problem, and you cannot see it in an accuracy figure.

85
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:18,600
On the right of the slide is a second view, the ROC curve, which comes from sweeping the threshold on one perceptron's score from very strict to very permissive and plotting what happens.

86
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:23,425
We will come to it in two slides, once the counts it is built from are clear.

87
00:07:24,025 --> 00:07:29,800
Everything in classification metrics comes from four counts, so let us fix them properly.

88
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:32,800
Pick one class and call it positive.

89
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:37,050
Every prediction then falls into exactly one of four boxes.

90
00:07:37,050 --> 00:07:40,950
True positives: it was positive and you said positive.

91
00:07:40,950 --> 00:07:41,900
Right.

92
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:45,875
False positives: it was negative and you said positive.

93
00:07:45,875 --> 00:07:47,775
A false alarm.

94
00:07:47,775 --> 00:07:51,750
False negatives: it was positive and you said negative.

95
00:07:51,750 --> 00:07:53,000
A miss.

96
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,800
True negatives: it was negative and you said negative.

97
00:07:56,800 --> 00:07:57,750
Right.

98
00:07:57,750 --> 00:07:59,450
Four numbers.

99
00:07:59,450 --> 00:08:04,550
And the different metrics are nothing more than different choices of what to divide by.

100
00:08:04,550 --> 00:08:11,425
That really is the whole difference between them, and if you hold on to that, you never have to memorise a formula again.

101
00:08:11,425 --> 00:08:19,850
Divide the true positives by the whole column of things you called positive, and you get precision: of the things I flagged, how many were real?

102
00:08:19,850 --> 00:08:28,150
Divide the true positives by the whole row of things that really are positive, and you get recall: of the real ones, how many did I find?

103
00:08:28,150 --> 00:08:32,100
Two vocabulary notes that cause confusion every year.

104
00:08:32,100 --> 00:08:37,100
Recall and the true-positive rate are two names for exactly the same number.

105
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:48,675
And the false-positive rate is the other row: false positives divided by all the true negatives plus false positives, that is, of the things that were not positive, how many did I wrongly flag?

106
00:08:48,675 --> 00:08:53,400
Get those two straight now, because the ROC curve plots one against the other.

107
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:58,375
Let me put our numbers on it, because the asymmetry is instructive.

108
00:08:58,375 --> 00:09:04,200
For the vertical class: recall nought point nine five, precision nought point seven six.

109
00:09:04,200 --> 00:09:05,575
Read that.

110
00:09:05,575 --> 00:09:11,725
Recall nought point nine five means we found ninety-five per cent of the real vertical bars.

111
00:09:11,725 --> 00:09:13,150
Excellent.

112
00:09:13,150 --> 00:09:20,575
Precision nought point seven six means that of everything we called vertical, only seventy-six per cent actually was.

113
00:09:20,575 --> 00:09:22,025
Why the gap?

114
00:09:22,025 --> 00:09:26,850
Go back to the confusion matrix: nine crosses were called vertical.

115
00:09:26,850 --> 00:09:30,725
Those nine are counted as false positives for vertical.

116
00:09:30,725 --> 00:09:41,900
They do not hurt vertical's recall at all, because recall only asks about the real verticals, but they damage its precision, because precision asks about everything we labelled vertical.

117
00:09:41,900 --> 00:09:48,975
So one number says the classifier is excellent at vertical and the other says it is mediocre, and both are true.

118
00:09:48,975 --> 00:09:51,675
They are answering different questions.

119
00:09:51,675 --> 00:10:00,200
Which you should care about depends entirely on what happens next, and this is a decision about the biology or the clinic, not about the model.

120
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:06,475
If you are screening, and anything you flag will be checked by a cheap follow-up experiment, favour recall.

121
00:10:06,475 --> 00:10:11,650
Missing a real hit is expensive; a false alarm costs one cheap test.

122
00:10:11,650 --> 00:10:20,725
If every positive triggers something expensive or irreversible, a structure determination, a patient being told something, favour precision.

123
00:10:20,725 --> 00:10:24,050
Now a false alarm is the expensive error.

124
00:10:24,050 --> 00:10:33,375
For completeness, for the cross class, recall drops to nought point seven five: the cross is genuinely the hard class, as we predicted from the pixels.

125
00:10:33,975 --> 00:10:38,975
Now the most important warning in this block, and it is about accuracy alone.

126
00:10:38,975 --> 00:10:45,375
Suppose I build a model that predicts whether a PhD student will eventually pass their defence.

127
00:10:45,375 --> 00:10:48,300
My model is: everyone passes.

128
00:10:48,300 --> 00:10:54,475
That model is right about ninety-nine point nine per cent of the time, because almost everyone does.

129
00:10:54,475 --> 00:10:57,600
It contains no information whatsoever.

130
00:10:57,600 --> 00:11:00,600
It is not a model; it is a constant.

131
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:04,625
The same thing happens in our data in a less obvious form.

132
00:11:04,625 --> 00:11:10,750
The subcellular localisation dataset has five classes, but they are not equally common.

133
00:11:10,750 --> 00:11:15,825
Always answering nucleus beats guessing at random, and it has learned nothing.

134
00:11:15,825 --> 00:11:23,100
Both of those models have a Matthews correlation coefficient of exactly zero, which is what MCC is for.

135
00:11:23,100 --> 00:11:32,100
So the rule, which I would like you to apply to other people's papers as well as your own work: never report accuracy alone on unbalanced data.

136
00:11:32,100 --> 00:11:36,650
Report MCC, or report precision and recall, next to it.

137
00:11:36,650 --> 00:11:42,125
And be aware that real data is usually far more unbalanced than a teaching dataset.

138
00:11:42,125 --> 00:11:56,500
If you are looking for a rare targeting signal that occurs in two per cent of proteins, then always answering no is ninety-eight per cent accurate, and if you report that number without comment, you are misleading your reader, whatever you intended.

139
00:11:56,500 --> 00:12:10,300
A practical note for the rest of this session: our localisation classes have been deliberately balanced to a hundred and forty proteins each, seven hundred in total, so that accuracy is interpretable while we are learning the machinery.

140
00:12:10,300 --> 00:12:13,225
Real proteomes are not so kind.

141
00:12:13,825 --> 00:12:20,175
Two single numbers that behave better than accuracy, and then we are done with classification metrics.

142
00:12:20,175 --> 00:12:23,175
The Matthews correlation coefficient.

143
00:12:23,175 --> 00:12:28,575
It combines all four counts into one number, running from minus one to plus one.

144
00:12:28,575 --> 00:12:30,775
Plus one is perfect.

145
00:12:30,775 --> 00:12:35,800
Zero is chance, including the always-say-yes model from the last slide.

146
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:41,325
Minus one is perfectly wrong, which is rare and usually means you have swapped your labels.

147
00:12:41,325 --> 00:12:50,775
Its virtue is that it stays honest when the classes are unbalanced, because it involves all four boxes rather than just the ones a lazy classifier can fill.

148
00:12:50,775 --> 00:12:56,700
On our three-by-three test set, the multi-class MCC is nought point eight seven one.

149
00:12:56,700 --> 00:12:59,725
The area under the ROC curve, AUROC.

150
00:12:59,725 --> 00:13:07,075
The ROC curve comes from sweeping the decision threshold and plotting the true-positive rate against the false-positive rate.

151
00:13:07,075 --> 00:13:20,875
The area under it has a beautifully concrete interpretation, which is the only one worth remembering: it is the probability that a randomly chosen positive example gets a higher score than a randomly chosen negative one.

152
00:13:20,875 --> 00:13:26,025
So nought point five is guessing, and one point zero is a perfect ranking.

153
00:13:26,025 --> 00:13:30,150
For our cross class, AUROC is nought point nine three eight.

154
00:13:30,150 --> 00:13:33,575
Notice what that measures and what it does not.

155
00:13:33,575 --> 00:13:39,550
AUROC is about ranking, not about any particular threshold, and not about calibration.

156
00:13:39,550 --> 00:13:44,875
A model can have a superb AUROC and still put its threshold in a useless place.

157
00:13:44,875 --> 00:13:55,900
F1 also exists and you will see it everywhere: it is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, which is a way of insisting that both are decent rather than letting a model trade one away.

158
00:13:55,900 --> 00:14:03,300
And one connection worth making: Session five's E-value cut-off was a point on exactly this kind of trade-off curve.

159
00:14:03,300 --> 00:14:07,600
Choosing a cut-off is choosing where on the ROC curve you want to sit.

160
00:14:08,200 --> 00:14:12,550
Regression now, which has fewer metrics and more ways to be misled.

161
00:14:12,550 --> 00:14:16,050
Three numbers, and each answers a different question.

162
00:14:16,050 --> 00:14:19,175
The root mean squared error, RMSE.

163
00:14:19,175 --> 00:14:22,500
Take each error, square it, average, square-root.

164
00:14:22,500 --> 00:14:26,825
Its virtue is that it comes out in the units of the thing you are predicting.

165
00:14:26,825 --> 00:14:37,650
If you are predicting helix fraction, an RMSE of nought point one means you are typically off by about a tenth, and you can decide for yourself whether that is good enough for what you want to do.

166
00:14:37,650 --> 00:14:40,300
No other metric tells you that.

167
00:14:40,300 --> 00:14:42,650
Always give it a baseline.

168
00:14:42,650 --> 00:14:48,725
The simplest model that could possibly exist predicts the mean of the training data for everything.

169
00:14:48,725 --> 00:14:52,225
For our data that scores nought point two three four.

170
00:14:52,225 --> 00:15:00,050
So if your clever model reports nought point two two, it is barely beating a constant, and you should know that before you write it up.

171
00:15:00,050 --> 00:15:02,725
Pearson's r, the correlation.

172
00:15:02,725 --> 00:15:07,075
It measures whether the predictions and the truth rise and fall together.

173
00:15:07,075 --> 00:15:09,700
It answers: have I got the trend?

174
00:15:09,700 --> 00:15:11,250
R squared.

175
00:15:11,250 --> 00:15:18,225
It compares your model against that mean-predicting baseline directly: what fraction of the variance did I explain?

176
00:15:18,225 --> 00:15:21,725
Zero means you did as well as predicting the mean.

177
00:15:21,725 --> 00:15:27,275
Negative means you did worse, which is entirely possible and should make you check your code.

178
00:15:27,275 --> 00:15:33,275
On the helix data, R squared is about a half, so roughly half the variance is explained.

179
00:15:33,275 --> 00:15:39,975
Three questions: how far off am I, in real units; do I have the trend; and am I better than doing nothing.

180
00:15:39,975 --> 00:15:43,050
You want all three, and they can disagree.

181
00:15:43,650 --> 00:15:50,800
Here is the trap in that list, and it is common enough in published bioinformatics that I want you to be able to spot it.

182
00:15:50,800 --> 00:15:53,150
Before I show you, predict.

183
00:15:53,150 --> 00:15:56,200
Take a set of predictions that are quite good.

184
00:15:56,200 --> 00:15:59,975
Now double every prediction and add nought point three.

185
00:15:59,975 --> 00:16:02,525
What happens to the correlation?

186
00:16:02,525 --> 00:16:05,250
Most people expect it to fall.

187
00:16:05,250 --> 00:16:07,525
It does not change at all.

188
00:16:07,525 --> 00:16:09,800
Not slightly: not at all.

189
00:16:09,800 --> 00:16:18,300
Correlation measures whether things move together, and doubling and shifting every value preserves the ordering and the relative spacing perfectly.

190
00:16:18,300 --> 00:16:20,950
The correlation is identical.

191
00:16:20,950 --> 00:16:30,100
Meanwhile, on the same transformed predictions, the RMSE roughly quadruples, because every prediction is now badly wrong in absolute terms.

192
00:16:30,100 --> 00:16:36,225
And R squared falls below zero, which means these predictions are worse than always guessing the mean.

193
00:16:36,225 --> 00:16:42,650
So you have one set of numbers that is simultaneously excellently correlated and worse than useless.

194
00:16:42,650 --> 00:16:45,475
This is not a contrived example.

195
00:16:45,475 --> 00:16:54,325
It is exactly what happens when a method is systematically biased, predicting everything too high, or when it has the right shape on a different scale.

196
00:16:54,325 --> 00:17:01,550
And it is why a paper that reports only the correlation of its predictions is not telling you whether its predictions are right.

197
00:17:01,550 --> 00:17:04,550
It is telling you they are in the right order.

198
00:17:04,550 --> 00:17:12,700
If you take one habit from this slide: when you report a correlation for a regression, report RMSE or R squared next to it.

199
00:17:12,700 --> 00:17:16,025
And when you read one, notice if they did not.

200
00:17:16,625 --> 00:17:22,975
Now we start on the ways evaluation goes wrong, beginning with the gentlest one: ordinary luck.

201
00:17:22,975 --> 00:17:24,725
Same data.

202
00:17:24,725 --> 00:17:28,075
Same model, a logistic-regression baseline.

203
00:17:28,075 --> 00:17:34,300
The only thing that changes is which proteins happen to land in training, validation and test.

204
00:17:34,300 --> 00:17:39,850
Ten different random splits, in the usual seventy, fifteen, fifteen proportions.

205
00:17:39,850 --> 00:17:45,550
The accuracies range from nought point five zero five to nought point six one zero.

206
00:17:45,550 --> 00:17:51,175
Mean nought point five six one, standard deviation nought point zero three one.

207
00:17:51,175 --> 00:17:55,225
Ten points of accuracy, from nothing but the luck of the draw.

208
00:17:55,225 --> 00:17:59,200
Sit with that, because it calibrates how to read everything else.

209
00:17:59,200 --> 00:18:11,150
If someone reports that their method scores nought point five nine and a competing method scores nought point five six, on a dataset this size, with one split each, they have shown you nothing at all.

210
00:18:11,150 --> 00:18:15,025
The difference is inside the range you get by reshuffling.

211
00:18:15,025 --> 00:18:18,975
One partial remedy is k-fold cross-validation.

212
00:18:18,975 --> 00:18:26,275
Split the data into five parts; train five times, each time holding out a different part; average the results.

213
00:18:26,275 --> 00:18:33,175
Every protein is validated exactly once, and your estimate no longer depends on one arbitrary division.

214
00:18:33,175 --> 00:18:38,925
Here that gives nought point five four zero, plus or minus nought point zero four four.

215
00:18:38,925 --> 00:18:47,675
It costs five times the computing, and, importantly, it reduces the dependence on one split but does not abolish sampling variability.

216
00:18:47,675 --> 00:18:50,425
Your dataset is still a sample.

217
00:18:50,425 --> 00:18:57,525
If it is small, your estimate is uncertain, and no amount of re-splitting creates information that is not there.

218
00:18:57,525 --> 00:19:00,275
Report the spread, not just the mean.

219
00:19:00,275 --> 00:19:05,400
A single number with no uncertainty attached is a claim you cannot support.

220
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:11,525
And now the one that is specific to our field, and the most important slide in the session.

221
00:19:11,525 --> 00:19:14,050
Proteins come in families.

222
00:19:14,050 --> 00:19:22,225
A random split does not know that, so it will cheerfully put one member of a family in training and its close relative in the test set.

223
00:19:22,225 --> 00:19:24,125
Let us measure it.

224
00:19:24,125 --> 00:19:34,625
Take our seven hundred proteins and cluster them with MMseqs2 at thirty per cent sequence identity, which is a conventional rough threshold for likely homology.

225
00:19:34,625 --> 00:19:39,300
The seven hundred proteins fall into five hundred and fifty-one clusters.

226
00:19:39,300 --> 00:19:48,075
Seventy-five of those clusters have more than one member, accounting for two hundred and twenty-four proteins, and the largest cluster has eleven.

227
00:19:48,075 --> 00:19:58,375
Now split at random and ask how much contamination that produces: thirty-one point three per cent of validation proteins have a homolog sitting in the training set.

228
00:19:58,375 --> 00:20:00,250
Nearly a third.

229
00:20:00,250 --> 00:20:03,000
And here is what it does to the score.

230
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:09,950
Those proteins with a relative in training are predicted correctly sixty-seven point five per cent of the time.

231
00:20:09,950 --> 00:20:14,650
The ones without are predicted correctly fifty point nine per cent of the time.

232
00:20:14,650 --> 00:20:21,975
Sixteen and a half percentage points, between proteins the model has a relative of and proteins it does not.

233
00:20:21,975 --> 00:20:25,350
Think about what the model is doing in those cases.

234
00:20:25,350 --> 00:20:29,275
It is not applying general knowledge of targeting signals.

235
00:20:29,275 --> 00:20:35,725
It is recognising the family, because relatives share sequence and usually share localisation too.

236
00:20:35,725 --> 00:20:48,125
That is a perfectly good way to get the answer right, and it is not what you claimed your method does, and it will not work on a protein family nobody has seen before, which is precisely the case where you wanted a predictor.

237
00:20:48,725 --> 00:20:53,850
The fix is simple to describe and you should do it in every project from now on.

238
00:20:53,850 --> 00:20:55,700
Cluster first.

239
00:20:55,700 --> 00:20:58,850
Then split the clusters, not the proteins.

240
00:20:58,850 --> 00:21:06,875
Every member of a family goes into the same side of the split: all into training, or all into validation, or all into test.

241
00:21:06,875 --> 00:21:09,650
No family straddles the boundary.

242
00:21:09,650 --> 00:21:12,125
The slide shows the effect.

243
00:21:12,125 --> 00:21:22,250
Over ten splits of each kind, baseline accuracy drops from nought point five five three with random splits to nought point five three six with homology-aware splits.

244
00:21:22,250 --> 00:21:28,450
Now, that drop is small, and I want to explain why rather than let you conclude the problem is small.

245
00:21:28,450 --> 00:21:37,800
In this dataset, four hundred and seventy-six of the seven hundred proteins have no homolog in the set at all, so most of the data was never going to leak.

246
00:21:37,800 --> 00:21:43,450
The leakage was concentrated in the two hundred and twenty-four proteins that have relatives.

247
00:21:43,450 --> 00:21:46,925
In redundant data, the gap is much larger.

248
00:21:46,925 --> 00:21:49,900
A whole proteome is full of paralogs.

249
00:21:49,900 --> 00:21:55,400
The PDB is full of the same protein solved many times, and of close variants.

250
00:21:55,400 --> 00:22:05,775
In datasets like those, the difference between a random split and a homology-aware split can be enormous, and papers have been published whose central claim does not survive it.

251
00:22:05,775 --> 00:22:15,500
From today, the course uses the homology-aware split: four hundred and eighty-six training, a hundred and five validation, a hundred and nine test proteins.

252
00:22:15,500 --> 00:22:22,025
Sessions nine and ten reuse exactly that split, which is why their numbers are comparable with each other.

253
00:22:22,025 --> 00:22:31,650
One honest limitation: no cluster at thirty per cent identity crosses the split, but remote homologs below that threshold can still be related.

254
00:22:31,650 --> 00:22:35,850
Thirty per cent is a pragmatic line, not a guarantee.

255
00:22:36,450 --> 00:22:39,050
Homology is our field's leak.

256
00:22:39,050 --> 00:22:46,425
There are others, and they share a shape: something that distinguishes training from test, which is not the biology you meant to learn.

257
00:22:46,425 --> 00:22:48,175
Batch effects.

258
00:22:48,175 --> 00:22:53,575
All the tumour samples processed in one laboratory, all the controls in another.

259
00:22:53,575 --> 00:22:56,225
The model learns the laboratory.

260
00:22:56,225 --> 00:22:59,825
This is the tank story with real consequences.

261
00:22:59,825 --> 00:23:00,850
Time.

262
00:23:00,850 --> 00:23:09,000
You train on proteins annotated before some date and test on proteins annotated after it, which sounds rigorous and often is.

263
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:20,350
But if you do it the other way round, or if the annotation of your test proteins was informed by methods trained on your training proteins, information flows backwards in a way that is hard to see.

264
00:23:20,350 --> 00:23:23,325
Preprocessing on everything at once.

265
00:23:23,325 --> 00:23:31,825
If you normalise your features, or select which features to use, using all the data before splitting, then your test set influenced your model.

266
00:23:31,825 --> 00:23:35,750
This one is extremely common and completely invisible.

267
00:23:35,750 --> 00:23:40,475
And unrepresentative data generally, which we started the block with.

268
00:23:40,475 --> 00:23:49,250
The gold standard against all of this is a blind test: somebody else holds the answers, and you submit predictions before the answers exist.

269
00:23:49,250 --> 00:23:59,900
In our field that is CASP, which you met in Session six: structures that have been solved but not yet published, predictions collected, scored when the experiments come out.

270
00:23:59,900 --> 00:24:05,450
Nothing can leak, by construction, because the answer did not exist when you predicted.

271
00:24:05,450 --> 00:24:10,375
That is expensive and slow, and it is why CASP matters so much.

